complication and that's why  that's one reason that missing data can  be very challenging  for your task and um also  before trying to distinguish the type of  the missingness you need to be sure that  you know enough about your data  the data is complete enough or is  balanced enough  and uh just maybe as a rule of thumb uh  you will find that in many cases people  already assume that the data is missing  at  random just to make life easier for  themselves but  obviously this is not an accurate  assumption  so how to deal with missingness um  well if we should uh and that means that  if you have enough information to do the  imputation or to take any action  obviously  one uh scenario is to drop or ignore  missing values and i particularly  mentioned the art of dropping because as  maybe it sounds trivial  but it actually is not and it can really  uh be very tricky and dangerous and  to be able to uh make this important  decision  whether to drop or ignore the missing  values or not you need to have a good  understanding on  many factors around your data like how  much information you would lose by  removing  or dropping those samples and  if there is another or better approach  to take  the other kind of action that you may  take is to impute  and that means to fill in the missing  values with some um  let's say guess or estimation and this  can be done uh generally in two ways  which is which are called single  amputation and multiple imputation the  single computation as a name suggests  uh simply uh provides us with one  version or one uh  imputed version of the incomplete data  so in this case the red  square shows the missing entries and  it's imputed by the single imputation  um multiple imputation does that  multiple times so simply instead of  having one  version or one imputed version of the  data now we have multiple inputed  version  and this is actually to account for the  uncertainty of our guess or estimate for  the missing values and if you wanted  